  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Fri Jun 12 2026\n\nHot temperatures will continue on Saturday with a ridge aloft in \nplace over central and southern Texas. Somewhat breezy south winds \nare expected given the pressure gradient across our area.\n\nA temporary shift in the pattern is expected to bring increased rain \nchances and cooler temperatures for the first part of next week. \nWith an upper ridge building over the eastern Pacific and a broad \nupper trough over the Great Plains, Midwest and Great Lakes, the \nridge over Texas will be suppressed to the south. With this setup a \ncold front will move south into the Big Country by Sunday afternoon, \nand may reach I-20 by evening. The front is forecast to continue \nmoving south Sunday night and should be just south of our forecast \narea by 7 AM Monday. The better chance of showers and thunderstorms \nwill be along and behind this front as it progresses south. With a \nbrief period of northwest flow aloft, an embedded disturbance is \nprogged to move southeast across our area late Sunday night into \nMonday. This could bring additional showers and storms, with the \nhigher rain chances shifting into our southern counties Monday \nafternoon. A rather moist airmass is progged with precipitable water \nvalues mostly in the range of 1.6 to 2 inches Sunday into Monday. \nThis will be supportive of heavy rainfall, and slow-moving or \ntraining convection would bring a potential for flooding. \nTemperatures will be cooler on Monday behind the front with \nincreased cloud cover. Highs Monday will be confined to the 80s, and \nsome locations may not reach 80. A few lingering showers and storms \nwill be possible on Tuesday for mainly the southern part of our \narea. \n\nThe hottest temperatures so far this season will be possible \nWednesday into late week, when a ridge aloft over the southwestern \nstates is progged to shift east across Texas. Highs are forecast to \nbe at or above 100 in portions of the Big Country and Concho Valley \nWednesday. Forecast highs Thursday generally range from 100-104 in \nthe southeast half of our area, to 105-108 across the northwest \nhalf. If temperatures get this hot and current forecast dewpoints \nhold in the mid 60s to around 70, heat indices would potentially \nreach 110 in portions of our area. This would bring the need for \nHeat Advisories/Excessive Heat Warnings.",
